<header class="page-head docs-head">
    <div>
        <h1>The theory</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            Predictive coding (Rao &amp; Ballard 1999; Friston 2005; Bogacz
            2017) frames perception and learning as free-energy minimisation.
            This is the mathematics every other project in the codebase rests
            on — and the reason its learning rules never need a backward pass.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="docs-body prose">
    <h2>The model</h2>
    <p>
        A hierarchical network has <code>L</code> layers. Each layer
        <code>l</code> holds a latent state vector <code>μ_l</code> and
        generates a <strong>top-down prediction</strong> of the layer below:
    </p>
    <pre><code>pred_{l-1} = f(W_l · μ_l + b_l)</code></pre>
    <p>
        where <code>f</code> is a non-linearity (Tanh by default) and
        <code>W_l</code>, <code>b_l</code> are the generative weights and
        biases. The <strong>prediction error</strong> at layer
        <code>l−1</code> is precision-weighted:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ε_{l-1} = Π_{l-1} · (μ_{l-1} − pred_{l-1})</code></pre>
    <p>
        <code>Π</code> is a scalar or diagonal <strong>precision</strong> —
        the inverse variance, that is, how much the network trusts that
        error. The whole network then minimises the <strong>free
        energy</strong>, the sum of squared prediction errors:
    </p>
    <pre><code>F = ½ Σ_l ‖ε_l‖²</code></pre>
    <p>
        In this framing the sensory input is not something to classify; it is
        something to <em>explain</em>. Perception is the process of finding
        the latent states that make the top-down predictions match it, and
        learning is the process of making the predictions better.
    </p>

    <h2>Two nested updates</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Inference</strong> is fast and runs per example: settle the
        hidden states by gradient descent on <code>F</code>, holding the
        weights fixed:
    </p>
    <pre><code>μ_l ← μ_l − η_s · ( ε_l − W_lᵀ · δ_{l-1} + λ_act · sign(μ_l) )
δ_{l-1} = f'(pre_{l-1}) ⊙ ε_{l-1}</code></pre>
    <p>
        The input layer <code>μ_0</code> is <strong>clamped</strong> to the
        datum; during supervised learning the top layer is clamped to the
        label. Everything in between is free to move until the network
        settles.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Learning</strong> is slow and happens after inference has
        settled: the weights update with a <strong>local Hebbian</strong>
        rule — no backpropagation:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ΔW_l = η_w · δ_{l-1} ⊗ μ_lᵀ
Δb_l = η_w · δ_{l-1}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Every term in that update is available locally at a layer: the
        product of its own prediction error and the activity of the layer
        above. This is the property the whole codebase exploits — the same
        rule trains a classifier, a memory, an agent and a cortex, because
        none of them needs to see beyond their own synapse.
    </p>

    <h2>Classification without a softmax head</h2>
    <p>
        There is no output layer with a softmax. To classify, the network
        clamps the input, tries each candidate label at the top, and keeps
        the one that settles to the lowest free energy:
    </p>
    <pre><code>c* = argmin_c  F( x, one-hot(c) )</code></pre>
    <p>
        The class that needs the least correction to explain the input wins.
        This <code>argmin F</code> principle reappears everywhere in the
        solution: the mixture-of-experts gates on free energy, the cortex
        readout uses it, and the LLM's conventional cross-entropy training is
        a deliberate departure from it.
    </p>

    <h2>Variants implemented in the core</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Idea</th><th>Reference</th><th>Where</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Hierarchical predictive coding</td><td>Rao &amp; Ballard 1999</td><td><code>Network</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Free-energy principle</td><td>Friston 2005</td><td><code>Layer.LocalEnergy</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Diagonal precision, L2 decay</td><td>Huang &amp; Rao 2011</td><td><code>UseDiagonalPrecision</code>, <code>WeightDecay</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Feedforward Gist warm-start</td><td>Haider et al. 2024</td><td><code>UseFeedforwardGistInit</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>L1 activity sparsity</td><td>Keller &amp; Mrsic-Flogel 2018</td><td><code>ActivityRegularization</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Temporal continuity</td><td>Friston 2008</td><td><code>TemporalNetwork</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        Each of these is a configuration switch on the same engine, not a
        fork: a diagonal precision changes how errors are weighted, the gist
        warm-start seeds the hidden states with a single top-down pass before
        the iterative loop, and the temporal variants inject the previous
        timestep's latent as a prior.
    </p>

    <h2>Why locality matters</h2>
    <p>
        Because learning is local and energy-based, the same engine
        generalises cleanly to modular hierarchies, temporal cascades,
        attractor memories, active inference and mixtures of experts — all of
        which appear in the
        <a href="/predictive-coding/architectures">Expanded</a> and
        <a href="/predictive-coding/cortex">Cortex</a> projects without ever
        introducing global backpropagation. It is also the property that makes
        the system biologically plausible: no neuron anywhere needs to know
        about the loss function of the whole network.
    </p>

